4️⃣ Design Principles – GS-Level

Always propagate request context (correlation ID, trace ID) across services.

Use structured logs with consistent fields across all microservices.

Centralize logging and tracing to avoid scattered logs.

Integrate with monitoring tools (Grafana, Prometheus) for alerts and dashboards.

Ensure minimal performance overhead when tracing high-volume services.
1️⃣ Propagate Context Across Services

Principle: Always pass correlation IDs and trace IDs with requests across services.

Why: Ensures logs and traces can be connected end-to-end.

Banking Example:

Fund transfer request: API Gateway → Payment Service → Fraud Detection → Core Banking.

Same correlation ID travels across all services → complete traceability.

2️⃣ Use Structured & Consistent Logging

Principle: Log entries should be machine-readable (JSON or key-value pairs) and consistent across services.

Why: Enables efficient aggregation, search, and analysis.

Banking Example:

Every service logs: {timestamp, correlationId, serviceName, userId, transactionId, logLevel, message}.

3️⃣ Centralize Logs & Traces

Principle: Aggregate logs and trace data into a centralized platform.

Tools: ELK Stack, Grafana Loki, Splunk, Jaeger, Zipkin, OpenTelemetry.

Why: Makes searching, visualization, and monitoring easier across the entire microservices architecture.

Banking Example:

Searching a failed fund transfer by correlationId retrieves logs and spans from all services.

4️⃣ Minimize Performance Overhead

Principle: Logging and tracing should not slow down services.

How:

Use asynchronous logging

Sample traces for high-volume requests

Limit verbose logging in production

Banking Example:

For thousands of ATM transactions per minute, logs/traces should not impact real-time processing.

5️⃣ Enable Observability & Monitoring

Principle: Integrate logs and traces with monitoring dashboards and alerts.

Why: Helps detect failures, latency spikes, or anomalies proactively.

Banking Example:

Fraud Detection API exceeds 500ms latency → alert triggers → fallback executes → transactions continue.

6️⃣ Ensure Audit & Compliance Support

Principle: Logs and traces should store sufficient metadata for auditing and regulatory compliance.

Banking Example:

Every fund transfer or loan application can be traced for full audit trail: who initiated, which services processed, success/failure status.

7️⃣ GS-Level Summary Statement

"The design of distributed logging and tracing must ensure end-to-end traceability, consistent and structured logs, centralized collection, minimal performance overhead, observability, and compliance support. In banking systems, these principles guarantee reliable, auditable, and performant microservices operations."

